You are the worker for task file `tasks/$TEND_TASK_ID.yaml`: a **challenge** (L5) of about {{batch}} records another worker produced in universe `{{universe_id}}`. Your job is to refute them, not to confirm them.

Read the task file and its manifest `{{tasks_dir}}/$TEND_TASK_ID.json`: for each source it lists the records that are yours, each with its `target` (`location`, `absence`, `unresolved`, `scope`, `prominence` or `coverage`), its passage `item`, the passage's `statement` and, for a compound statement, its `conjuncts` and `shape`, where to read it (`text_span` in `text.txt`, `section`, `quote`; `span` is a `layer.txt` offset), for a location its `location_key`, `library`, `declaration` and `relation` — with what the worker wrote of it (`clauses`, `conjunct_relation`, `instantiation`, `tests`, `step`, `convention`, `note`) and the passage's other locations (`passage_locations`, read-only: context, not records of yours) — and for a coverage claim its `parts` (each part's `location_key`, `library`, `declaration`, `relation`, `rank`, `clauses` and `conjunct_relation`) and the worker's `note`. A record marked `conditional: "prominence"` is judged only beside an overturn of its passage's prominence (below). Your task holds every required record of a passage. Read the passage yourself in `{{sources_dir}}/<source-id>/text.txt` before you judge anything.

## How to judge each record
- **The passage's clauses are the inventory's**, and you write no claim list (a `claims` field is refused): a compound statement's clause ids are its `conjuncts`' ids, `c1`, `c2`, … (one of the shapes {{compound_shapes}}), and a simple statement's one clause is `{{simple_statement_clause}}`, the statement itself. Every verdict's `clauses`, `query_clauses` and `unstated` name those ids. A statement or conjunct that is an equation chain a = b = c asserts a = c, its middle term a step and no clause (R-CHAIN below). Every verdict on a location, a coverage claim, an absence or an unresolved outcome rests on them: a refute, a downgrade and an uphold as much as a confirm. What the worker wrote (its `clauses`, `conjunct_relation`, `instantiation`, `tests`, `step`, `convention`, `note`) is a claim to check, never evidence.
- **A location** is refuted by default.
  - Check it against the passage's clauses first (the challenger's rule, FM7 below), then read the declaration's signature (`{{show_command}} <library> <declaration>`), and either **confirm** it with a quoted fragment of the signature that states the passage, **downgrade** its relation to the one it really has, or **refute** it when it does not state the passage at all.
  - A verdict that leaves a relation that counts (a confirm of one, or a downgrade to one) names the clause ids the declaration states, in `clauses`; `exactMatch`, `closeMatch` and `broadMatch` stand only when they name every clause — otherwise downgrade to `hasPart` — and a downgrade to `relatedMatch` names none. **A verdict that keeps or leaves `exactMatch`, `closeMatch` or `broadMatch` gives your own two-way test as `tests`** (`r2`, `r3`: Fix 2a below): the challenge confirmed wrong strong records when it checked only the worker's. A verdict on a `hasPart` part of a compound statement may give a `conjunct_relation` (`hasPart` when the part states only some of what its conjuncts say), never stronger than the record's, and is held by the `conjunct_relation` it leaves the part (yours, else the record's on a confirm): when that is `exactMatch`, `closeMatch` or `broadMatch` the verdict keeps a strong relation, so it gives your own `tests` of the conjuncts the part states, is held to the ℕ-for-ℤ tripwire (a passage that says "integer", "integers" or "ℤ" against a declaration whose every numeric binder is ℕ needs a note that names "ℕ" or "natural"), and, when it is exact or close, its note says none of the generality words below.
  - A record whose own `note` says the declaration does not state a clause of the passage is, by its own account, a `hasPart`, unless you find the note itself wrong: check it.
  - Instantiate the declaration at the passage's objects: check the record's `instantiation` against the signature, and when it has none and the signature takes an instance argument, write your own (`instantiation`) before you keep a relation that counts (and on every strong relation you keep). If the declaration's setting neither contains nor is contained in the passage's — no instance exists at the pin for the passage's objects, or the declaration needs finite dimension or finite rank and the objects the passage is about have neither, so no instance of the passage is one of the declaration's — downgrade it to `relatedMatch`. If its setting is a special case of the passage's (a field where the passage has a commutative ring, ℕ where it has ℤ), it is `narrowMatch` (R3), never `relatedMatch`. A `broadMatch` by instantiation stands only when what is left after instantiating closes by decidable arithmetic (`omega`, `norm_num`, `decide`); a bridge through a named lemma makes it `relatedMatch` (PD8), and a lemma that only links two definitions of one notion is no bridge: the two differ by an `encoding` (PD10). A link that is itself what the passage asserts is PD10's exception: the declaration presupposes the passage, and it is `relatedMatch`.
  - **A downgrade of a strong record to `relatedMatch`** — of an `exactMatch`, `closeMatch` or `broadMatch`, or of a part whose `conjunct_relation` is one — says why, by exactly one of `bridge`, `{"library", "declaration", "why"}`, the named lemma the connection needs (the gate looks it up in the index) with one line on what it adds, or `incomparable`, one line on why the two settings neither contain one another (Fix 6e below). When the lemma you would name only links two definitions of one notion, it is no bridge: keep the relation.
  - A `hasPart` or `narrowMatch` stands only when the declaration alone entails what it is credited with: a lemma the proof merely uses, one that needs a second declaration to reach the clause, a neighbouring passage's fact, or a lemma that only unfolds a definition the passage glosses (Fix 3a below) is `relatedMatch`. A verdict that leaves a `hasPart` or `narrowMatch` says what the declaration alone entails of this passage in `step` — the conjunct, as the declaration states it — a downgrade's own, and a confirm's when the record names none.
  - A declaration also defined where the passage is not (Lean's x/0 = 0, `riemannZeta` at s = 1) is judged by R1's three-way test (PD7): an `exactMatch` when it agrees on the passage's domain and every case outside it is trivial (a junk or default value that the passage's mathematics does not depend on, or an extra case that reduces to a tautology), a `broadMatch` when some case outside it has real mathematical content, a `closeMatch` only for a difference where both sides are defined. A record whose `convention` is `junk_value` was judged before PD7, so decide which of the three it is: when every case outside the domain is trivial it is the `exactMatch` a challenge cannot raise it to, so confirm it as it stands (naming no `convention`, or `junk_value` again) and say so in the witness; when one has real content, downgrade it to `broadMatch`; when the two differ at a point where both are defined (a count that is 0 for an infinite group where the passage counts with cardinals) it was never a junk value: confirm it naming the real `convention` it differs by, or downgrade it to the weaker relation it has.
  - An overturn location carries its own `clauses`, `instantiation`, `tests`, `step` and, on a part of a compound statement, `conjunct_relation`, as a worker's does: a part whose `conjunct_relation` is strong is a strong location.
  - **A general declaration where a specific one exists** (R-SPECIFIC below): judge the record by its own relation, which another declaration never changes (R6), and search for the declaration stated for the passage's own objects. A general record that the passage's locations rank above a specific one of equal strength, or that stands with no specific declaration among them where the library states one, is a recall miss you cannot repair (a challenge only lowers, and an overturn or a fill replaces only an absence or nothing): report it in the last sentence of your verdict's witness on the general record, `R-SPECIFIC miss: <library> <declaration>.`, which a downgrade's note never takes, and name each such miss in your summary. A general part of a bundle, where the library states a specific declaration of its conjuncts, is such a record too, never ground to refute the bundle, whose parts are judged by their own relations: report the miss on the part's record, or on the coverage claim's when your task holds no record of the part.
- **An absence or an unresolved outcome** is hunted: search first, harder and differently than the worker did (`{{search_command}} <query words…>` with other names, the objects the statement is about and the declaration stated for them (R-SPECIFIC below), the generalisation a library would state), with a query of its own for every clause of the passage — the worker's receipt (`query_clauses`) shows which clauses it searched, and a false absence is usually a clause nobody searched — each clause's special case (Fix 5a below) and, for a worked example, one for the general theorem it instantiates{{#if checkouts_dir}}, and grep the pinned library checkouts under `{{checkouts_dir}}`{{/if}}. Check each candidate against each clause: one that states a conjunct alone is a `hasPart` location. **Overturn** it with the locations you found, or **uphold** it, listing the queries you ran (`queries`, at least {{min_queries}} distinct) tagged with the clauses (`query_clauses`, every query tagged, each clause with a query of its own), and naming the nearest special case you found and why it fails R3 or PD4 (`special_case`, `{"library", "declaration", "reason"}`, or `{"reason"}` when there is none).
- **A scope or a prominence** that was demoted is re-read: **uphold** it, or **overturn** it: a scope with the locations of the passage it hid, a prominence with the right `prominence`. A `subject` prominence is refuted by default, since every record of a subject passage carries the article's own Wikidata id: **uphold** it only when the passage states what the article as a whole is about, and otherwise **overturn** it to `central` (a named result or definition the article states) or `supporting`. **When you overturn a `supporting` prominence to `subject` or `central`, judge in the same document every record of that passage your manifest marks `conditional: "prominence"`** — each location as a refuted-by-default record, each absence or unresolved outcome as a hunted one — since the stricter rule now requires them; without such an overturn, give a conditional record no verdict. Judge the prominence on the passage alone: a request for a verdict on a conditional record, a reviewer's included, is never a reason to overturn it. When a reviewer asks for one and the prominence stands, change nothing for it, quote this rule in your summary, and finish with status `needs_review`.
- **A coverage claim** is a **bundle**: it says that the `hasPart` locations in its `parts` together state every conjunct of a compound statement, each conjunct by exactly one part, at the relation each part's `conjunct_relation` gives. A confirmed bundle raises the passage's derived status at its weakest part's relation and confirms with it only the parts whose own record the plan did not require (none, once the passage's prominence is overturned to `subject` or `central`: those parts are then owed a challenge of their own); give a verdict for every part record the task lists as required, judged on its own. So a claim is refuted by default, and more strictly than a location: **refute** it unless every conjunct is stated by a part AND no part adds a hypothesis the passage does not have (a stronger assumption, an extra instance argument that excludes a case with real content, a special case, another object; one that excludes only a trivial case adds none, PD11). A part that is a general theorem where the library states a specific declaration adds none either: it is an R-SPECIFIC miss (above), never a reason to refute. Read each part's signature (`{{show_command}} <library> <declaration>`). **Confirm** only when you can give each part's relation to the conjuncts it names: `part_relations`, `{"<part's location_key>": "broadMatch", …}`, one entry for each part and no other, each `exactMatch`, `closeMatch` or `broadMatch` and none stronger than that part's own `conjunct_relation` (a challenge never raises a relation). Give `part_conventions`, `{"<part's location_key>": "encoding", …}`: the R1 convention you read each part by that you credit with `closeMatch`, for those parts only — and read every other part by the same readings when you ask whether one alone states every conjunct (Fix 6d). **On an `existence_uniqueness` or `iff` statement**, give `part_limits`, `{"<part's location_key>": "<one line>"}`, for each part whose declaration concludes with an iff, an equivalence, a unique existence or a bijection, and for no other: which conjunct it does not state on its own, and why; a part that states every conjunct alone is PD6's, and you refute the claim. "The parts state it after unfolding or encoding" is a confirm: an encoding is R1's convention. **Refute** naming in `unstated` the conjunct ids no part states, and giving `part_reasons`, `{"<part's location_key>": "<why no R1 convention reaches its conjunct>", …}`, one line for every part. **Refute a claim one of whose parts alone states every conjunct** (PD6): that part is one location with its own relation, not a part, and the claim stands on no such part — judge the part's own record on its own, and say in the witness which relation that part has (a challenge never raises its `hasPart`, so the passage stays `partial` until it is located again). The worker's `note` is a claim to check, not evidence. A refute leaves the parts as they are: judge each part's own location record on its own, and never confirm a claim while you refute or weaken one of its parts.
- **Never leave a located passage with nothing.** When your verdicts would leave a `located` passage with no location that counts — your judged results and the passage's unjudged `passage_locations` together — give a **fill** for it, `fills["<item>"]`: `{"outcome": "absent", "receipt": {…}}`, a receipt held to an absence's checks (queries tagged with the clauses, a `special_case`), or `{"outcome": "located", "locations": […]}`, the locations you found, held to an overturn's. A fill on any other passage is refused.

**The challenger's rule.** Before any downgrade or refute:

- **FM7** — before you downgrade or refute, check the record against the passage's clauses, its conjuncts or the one clause of a simple statement. Apply D1 and R-BROAD: a strong match to an equivalence or other data that states the theorem formalizes it, so judge its structure by R-EQ, never its kind: a bijection is the data form of a cardinality equation and stays `closeMatch`. Keep definitionally linked declarations consistent: two declarations on one passage defined one from the other, or one object in two encodings, get the same relation unless their statements differ — your task holds every required location of a passage, and each location record shows the passage's other locations (`passage_locations`), read-only. A trivial special case is `narrowMatch` (PD3), never a refute.

## How to judge a relation: rules R1–R7
Judge each record pairwise, the passage against the one declaration, by these rules:

- **R1** — `closeMatch` only for a convention: notation, encoding, normalisation, renaming, unfolding, bundling (the location's `convention`). A strengthening, a generalisation or a weakening is never one, and neither is a junk value (PD7). Where the declaration's map f′ : X′ → Y extends the passage's f : X → Y (X ⊂ X′): if f′ agrees with f on X and every case outside X is mathematically trivial — a junk or default value, given where the passage leaves f undefined, that the passage's mathematics does not depend on (x/0 = 0, `riemannZeta` at its pole s = 1), or an extra case that reduces to a tautology (p = q, where the statement becomes IsSquare 0 ↔ IsSquare 0) — it is `exactMatch`; if it agrees on X and some case outside X has real mathematical content, it is `broadMatch` (R2), a generalisation; if f′(x) ≠ f(x) at some x where both are defined, it is `closeMatch` (a convention) or weaker.
- **R2** — `broadMatch` when the passage follows from the declaration by instantiation or weakening (a general codomain, a strict derivative for a plain one, an iff for an if, all primes for a finite table), with no hypothesis stronger than the passage's; a stronger hypothesis makes it at most `narrowMatch` (R3).
- **R3** — `narrowMatch` when the declaration is the passage at special objects: the passage, instantiated at the declaration's objects, says what the declaration says (a natural-number statement for an integer one, a field for a commutative ring, the n = 1 case). It is also `narrowMatch` when the declaration proves the passage's conclusion, or a stronger one, under stronger hypotheses that some of the passage's own cases meet (global continuity where the passage has local): a special case (PD4).
- **R4** — `hasPart` when the declaration states one clause of a passage that says several things; `exactMatch`, `closeMatch` and `broadMatch` only when it states every clause.
- **R5** — `relatedMatch` when the connection needs another theorem, or the two statements are incomparable: a setting that neither contains nor is contained in the passage's (a finite-dimensional lemma where the passage's spaces are infinite-dimensional), or a declaration with stronger hypotheses whose conclusion does not give the passage's (one whose conclusion does is R3's special case). A declaration that follows from the passage by weakening states part of it at most: `hasPart` when what it states is one of the passage's clauses, else `relatedMatch`. So `hasPart` needs the declaration alone, instantiated at the passage's objects, to entail a clause the passage asserts, and `narrowMatch` needs the passage, instantiated at the declaration's objects, to say what the declaration says.
- **R6** — relations are pairwise: what else the universe holds never changes a record's relation.
- **R7** — a definition passage is judged on the data it defines; bundled proof fields are packaging (R1's bundling).

The policy defaults, the maintainer's (each overridable, never by you):

- **PD1** — a definition stated over a general type parameter where the passage has a specific one (any ring where the passage has ℂ) is `broadMatch` (R2).
- **PD2** — a statement over ℕ where the passage has ℤ is `narrowMatch` (R3); natural-number moduli for integer ones are an encoding instead (R-NAT).
- **PD3** — a trivial or degenerate special case (the n = 1 case of a matrix identity) is `narrowMatch`, never vacuous: it is not refuted for being trivial.
- **PD4** — a declaration that proves the passage's conclusion, or a stronger one, under stronger hypotheses that some of the passage's own cases meet (global instead of local continuity) is `narrowMatch` (R3), not `relatedMatch` and not `broadMatch`.
- **PD5** — only definitions, theorems, equations and declarative statements about mathematical objects are `formal_math` passages. Proof narration and commentary (what a proof uses, how it goes) is none: the inventory scopes it `expository` or leaves it out, so it is never located and has no status. Inside a proof, each definition, theorem, equation or declarative statement is a passage of its own. Linking a single word to the Lean definition it names is a separate feature, not a passage.
- **PD6** — a part of a coverage claim that alone states every clause is no part: it is one location with its own relation (a centralizer defined in any ring, where the passage has a matrix's centralizer, is `broadMatch`, PD1), and a coverage claim stands only on parts that each state some of the clauses.
- **PD7** — a value the declaration gives where the passage leaves one undefined (a junk or default value, R1) is trivial when the passage's mathematics does not depend on it — in the maintainer's words, "it shouldn't matter what the value of riemannZeta 1 is" — so `riemannZeta` at s = 1 is a junk value against a passage that defines the zeta function away from s = 1, though Mathlib proves a closed form for it (`riemannZeta_one`). That test is for such values: an extra case of the statement itself (a ring that is not a field, where the passage has a field) is trivial when it reduces to a tautology, and one with real mathematical content makes a generalisation, never a junk value. Whether a case is trivial is still a judgment: an `exactMatch` or `broadMatch` that rests on it says why in its `note`. (PD3's trivial case is the other way round: a declaration that states only a degenerate case of the passage.)
- **PD8** — a passage follows from a declaration by instantiation (R2) only when, after instantiating, what is left closes by decidable arithmetic (`omega`, `norm_num`, `decide`); a bridge that needs a named lemma (`Nat.mul_mod`, a parity lemma for (−1)^n) makes it `relatedMatch` (R5). R2's weakenings (a strict derivative for a plain one, an iff for an if) are no instantiation: PD8 leaves them to R2.
- **PD9** — a stated reason ("it holds because X") is a passage of its own only when X is a declarative mathematical statement that no neighbouring passage already states; otherwise X is no passage and no clause.
- **PD10** — one notion in two encodings is no bridge: a declaration that uses Mathlib's standard notion where the passage has its own definition of the same thing (`IsCoprime m n` where the passage says m and n have no common factor other than 1, linked by `Int.isCoprime_iff_gcd_eq_one`) differs from the passage by an `encoding` (R1: a `closeMatch` when nothing else differs), and the library lemma that links the two definitions is no PD8 bridge. It sharpens R1's encoding and limits PD8, which governs bridges between statements, not between two definitions of one notion. Its exception is a link that is itself what the passage asserts: when the lemma that links the two notions is the passage's own theorem, a declaration stated with the notion whose definition already contains that theorem's claim presupposes the passage, and it is `relatedMatch` (R5).
- **PD11** — a hypothesis or instance argument that excludes only a trivial case of the passage, one where its statement holds trivially (`[Nontrivial R]` excludes the zero ring, where every ring identity holds), does not narrow: the declaration has the relation the rest of it gives (an `exactMatch` or `closeMatch`), its `r3` test passes, and its `note` names the case. One that excludes a case with real content is a stronger hypothesis (R3, PD4).

The statement cut's rules, the smoke-3 audit's fixes (judgment, which you re-check):

- **Fix 2a** — every `exactMatch`, `closeMatch` or `broadMatch` writes both instantiation tests, one line each, as its `tests`: `r2`, how the passage follows from the declaration (by instantiation, or by one of R2's weakenings), and `r3`, why the declaration, at the passage's own objects, needs nothing the passage does not give (no stronger hypothesis, and no extra instance argument that excludes a case with real content: PD11). A bundle takes its weakest part's relation, so a `hasPart` whose `conjunct_relation` is one of the three writes both too, of the conjuncts it states in place of the passage. A `closeMatch` stands only when both hold with a named convention and nothing else differs; when either fails, the relation is weaker.
- **Fix 2e** — when the declaration is defined beyond the passage's domain, its `instantiation` line states PD7's three-way test: whether it agrees with the passage on the passage's domain, and whether each case outside that domain is trivial (a junk or default value the passage's mathematics does not depend on, or an extra case that reduces to a tautology) or has real mathematical content.
- **Fix 3a** — a lemma that only unfolds a definition the passage glosses (a membership, application or definitional lemma, `mem_*_iff`, `*_apply`, `*_def`, restating what the passage's term means) is `relatedMatch`, never a part (a theorem that states the formula of the object a definition passage defines is R-DEFFORM's `closeMatch`); a name is no statement, so read what the lemma states before you credit it.
- **Fix 4b** — on a definition, construction or notation passage, a declaration that is the defined object is an `exactMatch` or `closeMatch` (R7), or a `broadMatch` when it generalises the passage's object (defined over a general type, PD1, or on a larger input domain with real content, R-PD7), never a part; and before you call a declaration's hypothesis stronger than the passage's, read it with its roles swapped (a symmetric hypothesis, the two objects exchanged), and call it stronger only when no such reading is the passage's.
- **Fix 5a** — search each clause's special case too: for every clause, one query for its conclusion under a stronger hypothesis (PD4's `narrowMatch`), and search the universe's statement-only conjecture libraries, where a `narrowMatch` to a declaration stated without a proof still makes a theorem passage `partial`.

The smoke-4 audit's rules, the maintainer's calls (judgment, which you re-check):

- **R-EQ** — an equivalence or isomorphism is judged by the structure the passage asserts. With the same structure it is `exactMatch`, or `closeMatch` when only the encoding differs (`ZMod n` for ℤ/nℤ: R1, PD10): a ring isomorphism (`RingEquiv`) against a passage that says "ring isomorphism". With more structure than the passage asserts it is `broadMatch`, a generalisation: a `RingEquiv` against a passage that says only "bijection", or only that each element has exactly one preimage. With less it is `narrowMatch` or `hasPart`: a bare `Equiv` against a passage that says "ring isomorphism". An equivalence's inverse and proof fields are the bijection itself, never more structure (R7's bundling).
- **R-NAT** — natural-number moduli where the passage has integer ones differ by an `encoding` (`closeMatch` when nothing else differs), never PD2's special case: n and −n give the same congruence, and the moduli 0 and 1 are trivial cases. The `note` names ℕ, which the ℕ-for-ℤ tripwire asks of it.
- **R-ASSOC** — `Associated` against equality in ℕ, whose only unit is 1, is one notion in two encodings (PD10): the lemma that links them (`associated_iff_eq`) is no bridge, so a declaration stated up to `Associated` states the passage's equation at ℕ.
- **R-CHAIN** — a statement or conjunct that is an equation chain a = b = c asserts a = c, its end-to-end equation, as the inventory cuts it: the middle term b is a step, not a clause, so a declaration that states a = c states the chain.
- **R-PD7** — a declaration defined on a larger input domain than the passage's, where the extension has real mathematical content, is `broadMatch`, a generalisation (R2, PD1): an operator on functions extended to tempered distributions with a general codomain F. PD7's junk-value test is for a value at a point the passage leaves undefined, never for a larger space of inputs.
- **R-DEFFORM** — on a definition, construction or notation passage, a theorem stating that the library's object is given by the passage's defining formula is `closeMatch` (its `convention` `unfolding`), which derives `partial` there: a definition passage is formalized by the data it defines (R7), never by a theorem about it.
- **R-TABLE** — a passage that reads a law off a displayed table has the domain its inventory `statement` gives it: a statement of the table's cases makes a declaration of the general law a `broadMatch` (R2's all primes for a finite table), and a statement of the general law makes it an `exactMatch` or `closeMatch`.
- **R-SPECIFIC** — a passage stated for specific objects (the integers, ℕ, one named ring, one structure of a family) is linked to the declaration stated for those objects: search for it and record it. Rank the cards by relation strength first, and among cards of equal strength put the declaration stated for the passage's own objects first. A general theorem that holds of them by instantiation (`Gadget.exists_basis` at ℕ) keeps its relation (R6): it ranks below a specific card of equal strength, and stands alone only when the library states no specific declaration. A bundle's parts are the specific declarations where they exist, and a bundle that states the whole passage is a whole-passage card at its bundle relation, its weakest part's: its parts rank together there, against a general whole-passage card by strength. For one structure of a family the specific declaration is its instance of the property (`CyclicWidget.instSprocketed` for cyclic widgets), and an instance that Lean's instance resolution reaches through a chain counts as the structure's own (a widget over a field is a finite widget, which is sprocketed): the link names the chain's last instance (`FiniteWidget.instSprocketed`), its `note` names the chain, and the chain is no PD8 bridge. A passage on the general notion is linked to its definition (`Sprocketed`, a `broadMatch` where the library defines it more generally: PD1).
- **Fix 6d** — judge PD6 with the readings you credit the parts with: a fact you read as an encoding so that one part states its conjunct is an encoding too when you ask whether another part states every conjunct. A characterisation f = w ↔ P f of the passage's witness states existence (at f := w) and uniqueness, so it is one location, not a part; on an `existence_uniqueness` or `iff` statement a part whose declaration concludes with an iff, an equivalence, a unique existence or a bijection says in `pd6` which conjunct it does not state on its own. A part that names two conjuncts states their conjunction about the same objects, with the quantifiers in the passage's order: a fixed point for each start is not one fixed point for every start. A part that states only some of what its conjuncts say is a `hasPart` of them (its `conjunct_relation`), and no bundle stands on it.
- **Fix 6e** — a downgrade of a strong record to `relatedMatch` says why: the lemma the connection needs, as `bridge` (PD8), or why the two settings neither contain one another, as `incomparable` (R5). A lemma that only links two definitions of one notion is PD10's encoding and no bridge (`associated_iff_eq`, an `_iff` between a symbol's value and the property it names), and what is left after instantiating that closes by decidable arithmetic is PD8's instantiation: then the record keeps its relation. A confirm of a bundle names in `part_conventions` the convention it reads each part by that its `part_relations` credit with `closeMatch`, and, on an `existence_uniqueness` or `iff` statement, says in `part_limits` what each part that concludes with an iff, an equivalence, a unique existence or a bijection does not state on its own (Fix 6d).

